## Adding a new EmbeddingProvider

1. Implement the `EmbeddingProvider` trait in
   `crates/u-forge-core/src/ai/embeddings.rs`. The trait contract is
   documented in the file — copy the shape from `LemonadeProvider`
   (`lemonade/embedding.rs`).
2. There is no `EmbeddingManager`. Construct directly, wrap in a
   `BuiltProvider`, and register with `InferenceQueueBuilder::with_provider`.
3. Gate live-server tests with `require_integration_url!()`
   (`crate::test_helpers`). Canonical `make test` supplies its owned server;
   direct `cargo test` skips silently when no server is reachable.
4. **Never add `Mutex<T>` around synchronous work in an async embedding path.**
   Use `tokio::task::spawn_blocking` if CPU-bound work is unavoidable, or keep
   the provider fully async via HTTP.

## Adding a new TranscriptionProvider

1. Implement the `TranscriptionProvider` trait in
   `crates/u-forge-core/src/ai/transcription.rs`. Return the server's response
   text trimmed of leading/trailing whitespace; use `mime_for_filename()` for
   MIME detection.
2. There is no `TranscriptionManager`. To wire into `InferenceQueue`, expose
   via `ProviderFactory` or register directly with
   `InferenceQueueBuilder::with_provider`.
3. **Do NOT add `GpuResourceManager` unless you need GPU contention
   serialisation.** `LemonadeTranscriptionProvider` is intentionally simple;
   GPU contention belongs in `LemonadeSttProvider` / `GpuResourceManager`.

## Adding a new schema type

1. Create `{type}.schema.json` in `defaults/schemas/`.
2. Import that schema explicitly through **File → Import Schema…**, or call
   `SchemaIngestion::load_schemas_from_directory` and persist the returned
   `SchemaDefinition` with `SchemaManager::save_schema`.
3. Import data only after the schema is persisted; import never infers a type
   or widens the accepted fields from observed JSONL.

## Working with SQLite storage

All storage goes through `KnowledgeGraphStorage`. See
[rust-patterns.mdc](mdc:.rulesdir/rust-patterns.mdc) for the
`INSERT OR REPLACE` vs `ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE` design decisions — they are
load-bearing, not stylistic.

`KnowledgeGraph::new(db_path)` takes **one argument** (the directory path).
The SQLite file is created at `<db_path>/knowledge.db`.

## GitHub issue and PR workflow

### Triage and planning

1. Use [the repository issue tracker](https://github.com/th3raid0r/u-forge.ai/issues).
   Search open and closed issues and relevant PRs before filing. Reuse the
   canonical issue instead of duplicating a report or reopening a resolved fix.
2. Keep each actionable item independently reviewable. Record the problem and
   impact, affected revision and paths, evidence or repro, in/out of scope,
   acceptance criteria, dependencies, and the relevant Make targets. Record
   design decisions and changes to scope on the issue. A design gate or triage
   issue is not implementation approval.
3. For an audit, verify findings against the cited revision and separate
   observed defects from hypotheses and intentional behavior. File confirmed
   findings by independently actionable scope; group unconfirmed observations
   only as explicitly marked triage. Record resolved/rejected dispositions
   without reviving them as backlog. If GitHub is unavailable, report the
   blocker; do not create a parallel tracker in the tree.

### Implementation and review

1. Read the issue, latest comments, dependencies, current owner, and linked
   PRs before starting. Don't dispatch a second writer or pick up work from a
   triage issue. For a bug, capture a regression test that fails for the
   expected reason before fixing it.
2. Keep the PR scoped to the issue. When publishing implementation, open a
   draft early, link the issue with `Closes #N` if the PR fully resolves it
   (otherwise `Refs #N`), and record local Make results and remaining gaps on
   the PR. Do not close completed implementation work before verifying merge
   or equivalent resolution.
3. For autonomous work, `agent work` is reserved for scoped, worker-ready
   issues authorized for that queue. When available, use the matching
   `github-audit-queue`, `github-auto-mode`, `github-issue-worker`,
   `github-local-checker`, and `github-draft-review` skills for the assigned
   role. A worker requests local checks with `needs local CI` on the draft and
   records the exact head SHA and targets. After local checks pass, use the
   `ready for review` **label** to request independent review while keeping the
   PR draft. Recheck changed revisions rather than carrying over stale results
   or approvals. These labels do not grant dispatch, promotion, or merge
   authority.

